A read-heavy system (100k+ reads/sec) is primarily a **caching and fan-out** problem, not a query-tuning problem. The database is the system of record; most reads should never touch it.

```java
@Service
public class ProductService {

    private final ProductRepository repo;       // Postgres (primary + read replicas)
    private final RedisTemplate<String, Product> redis;
    private final Cache<Long, Product> local = Caffeine.newBuilder()
        .maximumSize(10_000)
        .expireAfterWrite(Duration.ofSeconds(30))   // short TTL → freshness + locality
        .build();

    // ---- Multi-layer cache-aside read path ----
    public Product findById(Long id) {
        // L1: JVM cache (hot product → nanoseconds, no network)
        Product cached = local.getIfPresent(id);
        if (cached != null) return cached;

        // L2: Redis (shared across pods → sub-millisecond)
        cached = redis.opsForValue().get("product:" + id);
        if (cached != null) { local.put(id, cached); return cached; }

        // L3: read-replica DB (milliseconds)
        Product fresh = repo.findById(id).orElseThrow();
        redis.opsForValue().set("product:" + id, fresh, Duration.ofMinutes(5));
        local.put(id, fresh);
        return fresh;
    }

    // ---- Write path invalidates both caches ----
    @Transactional
    public Product update(Long id, ProductDto dto) {
        Product saved = repo.save(dto.toEntity(id));       // writes to PRIMARY only
        redis.delete("product:" + id);                      // invalidate L2
        local.invalidate(id);                               // invalidate L1 on this pod
        eventPublisher.publish(new ProductChanged(id));     // other pods evict via pub/sub
        return saved;
    }
}
```

```yaml
# Read-replica routing — writes go to primary, reads to replica
spring:
  datasource:
    primary: { jdbc-url: jdbc:postgresql://primary:5432/app }
    replica: { jdbc-url: jdbc:postgresql://replica:5432/app, read-only: true }
```

- **Layer caches** L1 (local, 10ms TTL, 10k entries) + L2 (Redis, 5-min TTL, shared) + L3 (read replica). Each layer absorbs 80-99% of traffic from the next — the primary sees only writes and cold reads.
- **Read replicas** scale read throughput linearly; expect a few hundred ms of replication lag, so route real-time reads (user just posted a comment) to the primary.
- **CDN edge caching** (Cloudflare, Fastly) is the ultimate L0 for public `GET`s — response in 10 ms from anywhere on Earth.
- **Denormalize** for the hottest reads: pre-compute feed timelines, product recommendations, leaderboards — do not rebuild them on each request.
- Watch **cache stampede**: when a hot key expires, thousands of pods hit the DB simultaneously. Use single-flight locks, probabilistic early refresh, or stale-while-revalidate.

-- Schema with soft delete column
CREATE TABLE orders (
    id          BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    customer_id BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES customers(id),
    total       DECIMAL(12,2) NOT NULL,
    created_at  TIMESTAMP NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
    deleted_at  TIMESTAMP  -- NULL means active; non-NULL means soft-deleted
);

-- Partial index: only index active records, keeping index lean
CREATE INDEX idx_orders_customer_active
    ON orders(customer_id)
    WHERE deleted_at IS NULL;

-- Partial unique constraint: allow re-registration after soft delete
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_users_email_active
    ON users(email)
    WHERE deleted_at IS NULL;

// JPA Entity with automatic filter (Hibernate 6.x)
@Entity
@Table(name = "orders")
@SQLRestriction("deleted_at IS NULL")
public class Order {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private Long customerId;
    private BigDecimal total;
    private LocalDateTime createdAt;
    private LocalDateTime deletedAt;  // null = active
}

// Soft delete service method
@Service
@Transactional
public class OrderService {
    public void softDelete(Long orderId) {
        Order order = orderRepository.findById(orderId)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new EntityNotFoundException(orderId.toString()));
        order.setDeletedAt(LocalDateTime.now());
        // Dirty checking commits the update automatically
    }

    // To access deleted records, bypass @SQLRestriction with a native query
    @Query(value = "SELECT * FROM orders WHERE id = :id", nativeQuery = true)
    Optional<Order> findByIdIncludeDeleted(@Param("id") Long id);
}

// Pattern 1: Optimistic locking with @Version to detect stale writes
@Entity
public class Product {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private int stockCount;

    @Version  // Hibernate increments on every update; throws OptimisticLockException if stale
    private Long version;
}

// Pattern 2: Read-your-writes -- after write, return the saved object without re-querying
@Service
public class UserProfileService {
    public UserProfile updateEmail(Long userId, String newEmail) {
        UserProfile profile = repository.findById(userId).orElseThrow();
        profile.setEmail(newEmail);
        repository.save(profile);
        return profile;  // Return the object we just saved, not a replica read
    }
}

// Pattern 3: Idempotent event processing with deduplication
@Service
public class OrderEventConsumer {
    public void processOrderCreated(OrderCreatedEvent event) {
        if (idempotencyStore.exists(event.getEventId())) {
            log.info("Duplicate event {}, skipping", event.getEventId());
            return;
        }
        orderService.createOrder(event.toOrder());
        idempotencyStore.markProcessed(event.getEventId());
    }
}

// Pattern 4: Cache invalidation on write to avoid serving stale data
@Service
public class ProductCacheService {
    public void updateProduct(Product product) {
        productRepository.save(product);
        // Invalidate so next read fetches fresh data from DB
        cache.delete("product:" + product.getId());
    }
}
